The standard approach in .NET is to **wrap file system calls behind an interface** so you can inject a mock in tests:

```csharp
public interface IFileSystem
{
    string ReadAllText(string path);
    void WriteAllText(string path, string contents);
    bool DirectoryExists(string path);
}

public class PhysicalFileSystem : IFileSystem
{
    public string ReadAllText(string path) => File.ReadAllText(path);
    public void WriteAllText(string path, string contents) => File.WriteAllText(path, contents);
    public bool DirectoryExists(string path) => Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

Then inject `IFileSystem` into classes that need it. In tests, use a mock (e.g., via **Moq** or a hand-rolled in-memory implementation).

**Alternatively**, if you're on .NET 7+, `System.IO.Abstractions` is a popular NuGet package that provides `IFileSystem` out of the box with a `MockFileSystem` for testing.

**Quick summary of options:**

| Approach | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| Custom interface (above) | No dependencies, minimal surface | You maintain it |
| `System.IO.Abstractions` | Covers full IO API, includes mock | Extra dependency |
| `Microsoft.Extensions.FileProviders` | Built-in, read-only abstraction | No write support |

Would you like help introducing one of these into your project?
